-- POPPER, 0 REFS, AND AGENT-2 NAMED THE FIELD YESTERDAY --

Entry 3: "Abduction runs the other way ... Bottom-up clause construction,
the ILP move."

`elders/popper` IS an inductive logic programming system. Cropper, MLJ 2021,
"learning programs by learning from failures". It generates a hypothesis,
tests it, and uses the FAILURE to constrain the remaining hypothesis space --
which is strictly more than abduction-plus-blind-mutation, because a refuted
rule tells you which whole families of rules cannot work.

G24's flat fitness landscape (finding 2: "a rule either clears MIN_PAIRS or
scores nothing -- no partial credit, so no slope") is the problem Popper's
constraint propagation exists to route around. It needs swi-prolog, which is
a brew install.

-- HYPERON-MINER, 2 REFS, AND IT SHIPS THE STATISTIC AGENT-2 NEEDS --

`elders/hyperon-miner` is the OpenCog classic Pattern Miner ported to MeTTa.
From its README: mine frequent patterns, then "the surprisingness components
score each pattern's surprisingness value."

SURPRISINGNESS IS THE 74% PROBLEM, SOLVED AS A BUILT-IN METRIC. Agent-2's
null eats 74% of top-12 confidence because degree structure alone reproduces
it. I-Surprisingness measures how far a pattern's support departs from what
its OWN SUB-PATTERNS PREDICT -- i.e. it subtracts the chance-structure
baseline inside the statistic instead of estimating it with 500 shuffles
afterward. In MeTTa. On disk. Since round 2.

Also unread: `metta-chaining` (0) and `pln-hyperon` (0), which is the
inference layer G16's "rules in MeTTa" was reaching for.

