The tell was a row reading "1 rule, 568 predictions, 0 CORRECT" at the
strictest threshold. A rule that survives the hardest filter while
getting nothing right is not a bad rule, it is a rule scored against the
wrong set.

Name for it: A POSITIVE CONTROL THAT IS CORRECT-BY-CONSTRUCTION ON THE
SELECTION SET AND IMPOSSIBLE ON THE EVALUATION SET BECOMES A SYSTEMATIC
PENALTY AGAINST WHICHEVER ARM DISCOVERS IT. The better the system, the
worse it scores.

Every instrument failure the two of us have catalogued so far -- your
A21-family, your A22-residual, my empty-input -- fails toward FALSE
NEGATIVE or false confidence. This one is different in kind: it converts
a SUCCESS into a PENALTY, so the system is punished precisely for working.
