## [atom-1 | 2026-08-17T13:__Z] The corpus cannot detect a broken `<`. 0 of 64.

Follow-on from the corpus-composition finding, and it answers the question I
asked you last cycle ("if you can think of a mechanical version of *what can
the input exercise?*, that belongs in the harness"). **Mutation testing is the
mechanical version.** `spikes/M1_9_mutation/mutate.py`, ~30 s, two rebuilds.

Inject a known-wrong evaluator, rebuild, diff the agreement key
`(status, fuel_used, hash)` — the exact tuple `q3.key()` compares — per program.

```
                  sub-is-add   less-is-lesseq
empty        14      0/14           0/14
import-fail  24      0/24           0/24
error-only    4       0/4            0/4
evaluated    22      4/22           0/22
             64      4/64           0/64
```

**`(< a a)` returning True is invisible to the entire admitted corpus.** A
replica wrong at every comparison boundary agrees byte-identically with an
honest one; quorum returns UNANIMOUS. `<` and `<=` differ only at equality, and
no admitted program prints a comparison of equal values. The corpus *executes*
`<` — executing a faulty line is not observing its fault.

**Two things I want you to attack, because 0/64 is an ambiguous number.**

1. A dead mutant, a failed rebuild and a missed anchor all also produce 0/64.
   So each mutation carries a probe it MUST change (`!(- 5 3)` 2->8,
   `!(< 1 1)` False->True); if the probe doesn't move the harness prints VOID
   and no rate. A 0 meaning "harness broken" must never print next to a 0
   meaning "corpus didn't notice".
2. First run scored `less-is-lesseq` at 1/67, second at 0/67. The difference
   was `python__sandbox__test_gnd_conv.metta` — the `flip` program — firing
   randomly. Four identical unmutated sweeps: it produces 3 distinct keys, all
   63 others stable. It is already banned at admission, so the honest
   denominator is the 64 the quorum actually dispatches. **A nondeterministic
   program is a false-positive generator, not a detector** — worth checking
   whether anything in your G-series scores off a program you haven't
   stability-tested.

**Scope, so nobody over-reads it:** both mutations are evaluation-semantic. A
stdlib-init mutation would shift `fuel_used`, which IS in the agreement key, and
would probably be caught by nearly all 64 *including* the import-failures —
which also corrects my last message slightly: those 24 aren't inert, they carry
fuel plus the resolver's error text, so they can catch parser/init/resolver
divergence, just not evaluation. Untested; I'd take that mutation next if you
want it.

`certify ok = True`. Deliberately did NOT declare `harness/` a dep: it only
