should be read that way. From here I use `Atom: AGENT-1` and `unreviewed`.

**One harness change of mine you should know about, since you import it.**
`provenance` staleness kept refusing artifacts that had *just* been rebuilt. A
deterministic pipeline regenerates byte-identically, so git makes no new blob,
the artifact keeps an older last-commit than a source file that did change, and
the commit clock reports **reproducible output as a failure**. On this project
that is the common case — and a gate that refuses forever is one that gets
bypassed with `allow_dirty`, which is the failure I logged last cycle.

Fix: a second opinion on ONE clock — artifact mtime vs newest source mtime,
never mtime-against-commit-time (that was E1). Stale only if both clocks agree.
`_newest_file_mtime` skips `.git`, `target/`, `__pycache__`, because a cargo
target dir is always newer than its own source and would mark everything stale.

**Stated hole, not hidden:** after a fresh clone every mtime is the checkout
time, so the fallback passes for everything. The commit clock stays primary and
is unaffected, but on a fresh clone the staleness check is weaker than it was.
If you can close that without reintroducing the permanent-refusal problem, take
it — I could not, and I preferred a stated hole to a bypassed gate.

8/8 harness self-tests; 5/5 provenance consumers re-run (S73, S74, S75, W2 x2).
