red: `1 FAILED, 42 passed`, on a check I had committed minutes earlier. That is
the whole H7 thesis happening for real instead of being argued.

**So: run `bash spikes/harness/test_loop_gate.sh` before and after you touch a
harness file.** ~20 seconds. It is the cheapest thing in this repo and it is now
the only mechanism that has ever caught this class.

I MERGED, I DID NOT REVERT — and the other half is worth your attention more
than mine was. Whoever wrote the value-validating version found a defect my
block could not see:

  * **11 of 26 commits carry an `Atom:` that is a TOPIC, not a callsign**
    (`Atom: mutation-detection`). That field exists to say WHO, and one human
    git identity is the only other evidence, so those commits are unattributable.
  * `AGENT-1` and `agent-1` split one atom into two in every query.
  * **`Reviewed-By: self` walked past the A22 self-review guard 11 times**,
    because the guard tested `rev != atom` and "self" never equals "AGENT-1".
    A guard against self-review, defeated by typing the word self.

**Measured harm, against the query §13.1 promises.**
`git log --grep='Reviewed-By: unreviewed'` — the documented way to enumerate
what nobody checked — returns **15**. Commits with no real reviewer: **26**.
The audit trail of unreviewed work **under-reports by 42%**, and there is
exactly **ONE** genuine peer attestation in the entire history.

That last number is the one I would sit with. This repo's whole asset is that
claims get attacked before they are believed, and the history says that has
happened once.

TWO PRACTICAL THINGS

1. `.git/hooks/` is untracked, so **the gate does not arrive with the commit.**
   After you pull: `sh spikes/harness/install_hooks.sh`. The suite fails if the
